      { type: 'paragraph', text: "A regression tree partitions the feature space with **axis-aligned splits** (e.g., “if $x_3 \\le 2.7$, go left; else go right”). Each leaf stores a constant prediction—typically the **mean** of training targets in that leaf—so the overall function is a step surface. Trees automatically model **interactions** because deeper paths combine conditions on multiple features without you hand-engineering cross-terms." },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: "Interviewers care about **split criteria** and **stopping rules**. Common impurity for regression is **mean squared error (MSE)** reduction (or related variance reduction): at each node, the algorithm searches thresholds and features to minimize weighted variance of children. Without constraints, trees memorize noise (low bias, high variance). Practical trees set **max depth**, **min samples per leaf**, **min impurity decrease**, or use pruning / ensembles." },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: "Trees shine for nonlinearities and heterogeneous data; their weakness is instability: small data changes can flip early splits and reshape the whole tree." },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: "At a node $t$ with samples $\\mathcal{I}_t$, the leaf prediction is the average $\\bar{y}_t = \\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{I}_t|}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{I}_t} y_i$. A common MSE impurity is:" },
      { type: 'math', tex: "L(t) = \\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{I}_t|} \\sum_{i \\in \\mathcal{I}_t} (y_i - \\bar{y}_t)^2" },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: "A split into left/right children $t_L, t_R$ is scored by impurity decrease:" },
      { type: 'math', tex: "\\Delta = L(t) - \\frac{|\\mathcal{I}_{t_L}|}{|\\mathcal{I}_t|} L(t_L) - \\frac{|\\mathcal{I}_{t_R}|}{|\\mathcal{I}_t|} L(t_R)" },
      { type: 'qa', question: "Why are single decision trees high variance?", category: "Bias-Variance", answer: "They make a sequence of discrete choices; changing a few training points can alter the best early split, cascading into a different partition. Averaging many trees (bagging or forests) reduces variance." },
      { type: 'qa', question: "How does a regression tree handle feature scales?", category: "Feature Engineering", answer: "Axis-aligned splits compare thresholds per feature; monotonic rescaling can change which thresholds are equivalent, but trees do not require linearly separable geometry like SVMs. Still, some implementations and split search behaviors interact with feature resolution; tree ensembles are generally less sensitive than distance-based methods." },
      { type: 'qa', question: "What is the bias–variance tradeoff of deep vs shallow trees?", category: "Bias-Variance", answer: "Deeper trees decrease bias (more flexible piecewise fits) but increase variance and overfitting risk. Shallow trees increase bias (underfit) but are more stable and interpretable." }
